window.docContents[172].resolve({"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___4___-Filtrations/Existence-of-filtration-with-irreducible-successive-quotients/#Chapter3___Lemma3___4___2":{"contents":"Lemma 3.4.2. Any finite dimensional representation V of an algebra A admits a finite filtration 0 = V_0 \\subset V_1 \\subset \\cdots \\subset V_n = V such that the successive quotients V_i / V_{i-1} are irreducible.\n\nProof. The proof is by induction in \\dim(V). The base is clear, and only the induction step needs to be justified. Pick an irreducible subrepresentation V_1 \\subset V, and consider the representation U = V/V_1. Then by the induction assumption U has a filtration 0 = U_0 \\subset U_1 \\subset \\cdots \\subset U_{n-1} = U such that U_i / U_{i-1} are irreducible. Define V_i for i \\geq 2 to be the preimages of U_{i-1} under the tautological projection V \\to V/V_1 = U. Then 0 = V_0 \\subset V_1 \\subset V_2 \\subset \\cdots \\subset V_n = V is a filtration of V with the desired property. \\square\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t3. General results of representation theory\t3.4. Filtrations\tExistence of filtration with irreducible successive quotients","header":"Existence of filtration with irreducible successive quotients","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___4___-Filtrations/Existence-of-filtration-with-irreducible-successive-quotients/#Chapter3___Lemma3___4___2"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___6___-Unitary-representations___-Another-proof-of-Maschke___s-theorem-for-complex-representations/Section-4___6___-Unitary-representations/#Chapter4___Introduction_4___6___heading-1":{"contents":"","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t4. Representations of finite groups: Basic results\t4.6. Unitary representations. Another proof of Maschke's theorem for complex representations\tSection 4.6: Unitary representations","header":"4.6. Unitary representations. Another proof of Maschke's theorem for complex representations","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___6___-Unitary-representations___-Another-proof-of-Maschke___s-theorem-for-complex-representations/Section-4___6___-Unitary-representations/#Chapter4___Introduction_4___6___heading-1"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___8___-Induced-representations/Induced-representation-via-idempotent-e_chi/#Chapter5___Exercise5___8___5":{"contents":"Exercise 5.8.5. Let K \\subset G be finite groups, and let \\chi : K \\to \\mathbb{C}^* be a homomorphism. Let \\mathbb{C}_\\chi be the corresponding 1-dimensional representation of K. Let\n\ne_\\chi = \\frac{1}{|K|} \\sum_{g \\in K} \\chi(g)^{-1} g \\in \\mathbb{C}[K]\n\nbe the idempotent corresponding to \\chi. Show that the G-representation \\operatorname{Ind}_K^G \\mathbb{C}_\\chi is naturally isomorphic to \\mathbb{C}[G] e_\\chi (with G acting by left multiplication).\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t5. Representations of finite groups: Further results\t5.8. Induced representations\tInduced representation via idempotent e_chi","header":"Induced representation via idempotent e_chi","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___8___-Induced-representations/Induced-representation-via-idempotent-e_chi/#Chapter5___Exercise5___8___5"},"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___1___-Lifting-of-idempotents/#chapter-09___section-9-1":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t9. Structure of finite dimensional algebras","header":"9.1. Lifting of idempotents","id":"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___1___-Lifting-of-idempotents/#chapter-09___section-9-1"}});
